grep - find lines that match

SYNOPSIS
    grep [-ivnclrF] PATTERN [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
      -i   ignore case
      -v   print the lines that do not match
      -n   put the line number in front
      -c   print how many matched, not which
      -l   print only the names of files with a match
      -r   search a directory and everything under it
      -F   the pattern is a literal string, not an expression

    The pattern is a regular expression:

      .           any one character
      [abc] [^a]  a set, with a-z ranges and a leading ^ to invert
      * + ?       none or more, one or more, none or one
      {n} {n,} {n,m}   exactly, at least, between
      |           either side, and ( ) to group
      ^ $         the start and the end of the line
      \d \w \s    a digit, a word character, whitespace; \D \W \S for not

    No backreferences: they need the engine to remember what a group
    matched, and are the feature that makes a regex language stop being
    regular.

    Matching is by backtracking, so a pattern built to be slow can be.
    There is a step limit rather than a cleverer engine, because a
    cleverer engine is a much larger program and nothing here searches
    input chosen by an adversary.

SEE ALSO
    find, sort, uniq, sh
